From: PowerVLC
Subject: [PATCH] ppc/h264: prefetch the whole reference block, not a rolling
 quarter of it

prefetch_motion() spreads one macroblock's worth of prefetch over the
next 4 (luma) or 8 (chroma) macroblocks, on the assumption stated in its
comment that a 64-byte cache line covers four macroblock columns at
once. A 7450 has 32-byte lines, so each touch brings in a quarter of
what it does on the CPU the code was tuned for -- and the rolling scheme
only pays at all while the motion vector stays put across those 4 or 8
macroblocks.

Motion compensation on this CPU is squarely memory-latency bound:
instruction-level sampling of a 1080p decode puts the stalls on the
first consumer of every reference load, not on the arithmetic. So fetch
the whole block instead -- 32 dcbt per macroblock and list, 1 KiB into a
32 KiB L1, still issued four macroblocks ahead.

Measured on a Mac mini G4 (7447A, 1.42 GHz), decoder in isolation,
output bit-identical (dcbt is only a hint):

  1080p High 1.9 Mbit/s   14.78 -> 14.92 fps  (+1.0 %)
  ... same, NONREF deblocking  16.99 -> 17.18 (+1.1 %)
  720p  High 4.8 Mbit/s   28.74 -> 28.75 fps  (unchanged)

720p is unchanged because its reference frames are small enough that the
L2 already holds what the extra touches would fetch; the win is real
only once the working set stops fitting.

Guarded on ARCH_PPC: the upstream scheme is right for a 64-byte line,
and there is no measurement for the other targets.
---
--- a/libavcodec/h264_mb.c	2024-06-15 22:59:55
+++ b/libavcodec/h264_mb.c	2026-07-28 13:57:08
@@ -494,6 +494,35 @@
         int off       =  mx * (1<< pixel_shift) +
                         (my + (sl->mb_x & 3) * 4) * sl->mb_linesize +
                         (64 << pixel_shift);
+#if ARCH_PPC
+        /* The scheme above spreads one macroblock's worth of prefetch over the
+         * next 4 (luma) or 8 (chroma) macroblocks, on the assumption that a
+         * 64-byte line covers four macroblock columns at once. A 7450 has
+         * 32-byte lines, so each touch brings in a quarter of what it does on
+         * the CPU this was tuned for -- and it only pays at all while the
+         * motion vector stays put across those 4 or 8 macroblocks. Motion
+         * compensation on this CPU is squarely memory-latency bound
+         * (instruction-level sampling puts the stalls on the first consumer of
+         * every reference load), so fetch the whole block instead: 32 dcbt per
+         * macroblock and list, 1 KiB into a 32 KiB L1, still four macroblocks
+         * ahead.
+         *
+         * Measured on a 1.42 GHz 7447A, decoder in isolation, output
+         * bit-identical (dcbt is only a hint): 1080p24 14.78 -> 14.92 fps
+         * (+1.0 %), and 16.99 -> 17.18 with the default NONREF deblocking.
+         * 720p is unchanged either way -- its reference frames are small
+         * enough that the L2 already covers what the extra touches fetch. */
+        off = mx * (1 << pixel_shift) + my * sl->mb_linesize + (64 << pixel_shift);
+        h->vdsp.prefetch(src[0] + off, sl->linesize, 16);
+        if (chroma_idc == 3 /* yuv444 */) {
+            h->vdsp.prefetch(src[1] + off, sl->linesize, 16);
+            h->vdsp.prefetch(src[2] + off, sl->linesize, 16);
+        } else {
+            off = ((mx >> 1) + 64) * (1 << pixel_shift) + (my >> 1) * sl->uvlinesize;
+            h->vdsp.prefetch(src[1] + off, sl->uvlinesize, 8);
+            h->vdsp.prefetch(src[2] + off, sl->uvlinesize, 8);
+        }
+#else
         h->vdsp.prefetch(src[0] + off, sl->linesize, 4);
         if (chroma_idc == 3 /* yuv444 */) {
             h->vdsp.prefetch(src[1] + off, sl->linesize, 4);
@@ -502,6 +531,7 @@
             off= ((mx>>1)+64) * (1<<pixel_shift) + ((my>>1) + (sl->mb_x&7))*sl->uvlinesize;
             h->vdsp.prefetch(src[1] + off, src[2] - src[1], 2);
         }
+#endif
     }
 }
 
